1
00:00:05,441 --> 00:00:09,600
Jay Gatsby throws huge parties to win back Daisy Buchanan.

2
00:00:09,911 --> 00:00:15,603
This guide covers the novel and a version where he simply knocks on a door and asks her to tea.

3
00:00:16,495 --> 00:00:20,390
Hello, and welcome to Unwritten Chapters, Study Edition.

4
00:00:20,581 --> 00:00:25,450
In the next few minutes, I'll walk you through The Great Gatsby, by F. Scott Fitzgerald.

5
00:00:25,667 --> 00:00:30,104
It's a quick guide you can use for class, a book club, or your own curiosity.

6
00:00:30,316 --> 00:00:34,391
The full guide, with ten museum and archive sources, is linked below.

7
00:00:36,642 --> 00:00:44,574
Nick Carraway moves to West Egg, on Long Island, and rents a small house next to the mansion of the mysterious Jay Gatsby.

8
00:00:44,789 --> 00:00:49,422
Across the bay lives Nick's cousin Daisy and her husband, Tom Buchanan.

9
00:00:49,866 --> 00:00:53,022
Gatsby throws giant parties every weekend.

10
00:00:53,227 --> 00:01:01,058
Nick learns that Gatsby and Daisy loved each other five years earlier, before the war, and that everything Gatsby owns is meant

11
00:01:01,058 --> 00:01:02,295
to win her back.

12
00:01:02,583 --> 00:01:05,512
Nick arranges for them to meet again over tea.

13
00:01:05,885 --> 00:01:08,838
Gatsby wants to repeat the past exactly.

14
00:01:09,136 --> 00:01:14,767
The summer ends in a car accident, a death, and a funeral almost nobody attends.

15
00:01:15,001 --> 00:01:20,263
Nick goes home to the Midwest, thinking about the green light at the end of Daisy's dock.

16
00:01:22,058 --> 00:01:24,079
Now for the story.

17
00:01:24,292 --> 00:01:28,468
Our sleep story for this book is called "Tea Before the Parties."

18
00:01:28,591 --> 00:01:31,435
Here's how it unfolds, chapter by chapter.

19
00:01:31,901 --> 00:01:32,983
Chapter one.

20
00:01:33,128 --> 00:01:34,348
The Green Light.

21
00:01:35,020 --> 00:01:38,598
The story is told by a young man called Nick Carraway.

22
00:01:38,964 --> 00:01:42,628
He's come east from the Middle West to sell bonds in New York.

23
00:01:43,051 --> 00:01:47,782
He's rented a small, cheap house on Long Island, in a place called West Egg.

24
00:01:48,672 --> 00:01:52,074
The book begins with some advice his father once gave him.

25
00:01:52,758 --> 00:01:57,959
He means be slow to judge people, because you don't know what their lives have been like.

26
00:01:58,206 --> 00:02:00,050
Nick tries to live by that.

27
00:02:00,278 --> 00:02:04,115
It makes him a good listener, and that's why people tell him things.

28
00:02:04,496 --> 00:02:08,150
Nick's little house sits right beside an enormous one.

29
00:02:08,427 --> 00:02:16,083
It's a huge, glittering mansion with a tower, a marble swimming pool, and more than forty acres of lawn and garden.

30
00:02:16,497 --> 00:02:22,367
It belongs to a mysterious man named Gatsby, and nobody seems to know anything about him.

31
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:24,424
Chapter two.

32
00:02:24,650 --> 00:02:26,107
Tea in the Rain.

33
00:02:26,909 --> 00:02:29,867
The next morning, there was a knock at Nick's door.

34
00:02:30,534 --> 00:02:38,134
It was Gatsby, tanned and elegant in a beautiful pale suit, holding his hat in both hands like a schoolboy.

35
00:02:38,227 --> 00:02:40,269
He'd never been to Nick's house before.

36
00:02:40,533 --> 00:02:42,790
Nick thought he looked very nervous.

37
00:02:43,110 --> 00:02:45,374
He said he had a favour to ask.

38
00:02:45,652 --> 00:02:53,210
Nick's cousin, Mrs Buchanan, was an old friend of his from a long time ago, and he'd very much like to see her again.

39
00:02:53,574 --> 00:02:56,731
He didn't want it to be at a party or at the big house.

40
00:02:56,923 --> 00:03:03,228
He wanted it to be quiet, just tea at Nick's little cottage, if Nick would be kind enough to invite her.

41
00:03:03,992 --> 00:03:05,368
Nick said yes.

42
00:03:06,049 --> 00:03:10,385
On the day of the tea, it rained.

43
00:03:10,562 --> 00:03:15,577
In fact it poured, a heavy, warm summer rain that came down all morning and drummed on the roof.

44
00:03:16,617 --> 00:03:17,727
Chapter three.

45
00:03:17,934 --> 00:03:18,955
The Past.

46
00:03:19,878 --> 00:03:23,407
That summer, there were no parties at Gatsby's house.

47
00:03:24,162 --> 00:03:27,814
The orchestras never came, and the lawn stayed quiet.

48
00:03:28,247 --> 00:03:33,412
On Saturday nights the great house stood dark, with only one or two windows lit.

49
00:03:33,904 --> 00:03:40,672
People drove out from the city hoping for champagne, found the gates shut, and drove away again, disappointed.

50
00:03:41,448 --> 00:03:48,313
Instead, on warm afternoons, a small car would come across the bay from East Egg, and Daisy would get out.

51
00:03:48,672 --> 00:03:55,828
She and Gatsby and Nick would sit on the terrace, or walk down to the water, or take Gatsby's little boat out on the bay.

52
00:03:56,194 --> 00:03:58,971
They talked about Louisville, where Daisy grew up.

53
00:03:59,204 --> 00:04:06,311
They talked about the summer they'd fallen in love, five years before, when Gatsby was a young officer with no money and one

54
00:04:06,311 --> 00:04:07,020
uniform.

55
00:04:07,306 --> 00:04:09,721
Gatsby was very happy that summer.

56
00:04:09,993 --> 00:04:11,645
But Nick noticed something.

57
00:04:12,468 --> 00:04:13,904
The Last Page.

58
00:04:14,570 --> 00:04:18,001
Gatsby closed up the great house at the end of the summer.

59
00:04:18,802 --> 00:04:21,834
He told Nick he was going west to start again.

60
00:04:22,144 --> 00:04:25,261
He wasn't going to get rich, because he already was.

61
00:04:25,623 --> 00:04:29,795
He just wanted to find out who he was when he wasn't waiting for anyone.

62
00:04:30,302 --> 00:04:34,490
He said goodbye to Daisy on the terrace in the late September sunshine.

63
00:04:34,781 --> 00:04:39,862
He did it very kindly, the way you say goodbye to someone you'll always be glad you knew.

64
00:04:40,243 --> 00:04:42,418
She cried a little, and so did he.

65
00:04:43,315 --> 00:04:52,238
Then, early one misty morning, his car went down the drive, past Nick's little house, and out onto the road, and he was gone.

66
00:04:54,322 --> 00:04:55,533
Nick Carraway.

67
00:04:55,676 --> 00:04:59,936
The narrator, a young bond salesman who tries not to judge people.

68
00:05:00,359 --> 00:05:01,589
Jay Gatsby.

69
00:05:01,758 --> 00:05:06,786
A rich, mysterious man who's spent five years trying to win back Daisy.

70
00:05:07,187 --> 00:05:08,543
Daisy Buchanan.

71
00:05:08,736 --> 00:05:11,525
Nick's cousin, Gatsby's old love.

72
00:05:12,379 --> 00:05:13,663
Tom Buchanan.

73
00:05:13,799 --> 00:05:16,556
Daisy's wealthy, bullying husband.

74
00:05:17,347 --> 00:05:20,495
Pammy. Daisy and Tom's young daughter.

75
00:05:22,725 --> 00:05:23,729
The past.

76
00:05:23,949 --> 00:05:26,597
Gatsby believes you can repeat the past.

77
00:05:26,853 --> 00:05:28,823
The book suggests you can't.

78
00:05:29,654 --> 00:05:31,179
The American Dream.

79
00:05:31,558 --> 00:05:34,139
Gatsby rebuilt himself from nothing.

80
00:05:34,428 --> 00:05:38,305
Fitzgerald asks whether money can buy what he really wants.

81
00:05:38,751 --> 00:05:39,920
Appearances.

82
00:05:40,181 --> 00:05:44,407
The parties, the shirts and the mansion are all part of a show.

83
00:05:45,234 --> 00:05:46,309
Letting go.

84
00:05:46,625 --> 00:05:51,358
Our version imagines Gatsby choosing to stop reaching for the green light.

85
00:05:53,579 --> 00:05:54,950
So what's the what-if?

86
00:05:55,137 --> 00:05:57,252
Here's the turning point we changed.

87
00:05:57,762 --> 00:06:04,538
In our story, Gatsby asks Nick for the tea in June, in person, with no parties first.

88
00:06:04,948 --> 00:06:10,487
The meeting goes the same way, awkward then joyful, but the summer stays quiet.

89
00:06:11,007 --> 00:06:17,425
Everything after that moment is new, but the characters still act the way their authors wrote them.

90
00:06:19,450 --> 00:06:22,490
Before we go, here's a little about who wrote it.

91
00:06:23,229 --> 00:06:27,677
F. Scott Fitzgerald was born in Minnesota in 1896.

92
00:06:27,965 --> 00:06:34,698
He went to Princeton, served in the army during the First World War, and became famous young with his first novel.

93
00:06:34,996 --> 00:06:38,545
He and his wife Zelda became symbols of the Jazz Age.

94
00:06:38,909 --> 00:06:45,686
The Great Gatsby sold only modestly while he was alive, and it became a classic after his death in 1940.

95
00:06:48,036 --> 00:06:55,030
The story takes place in 1922, during Prohibition, when alcohol was banned in the United States.

96
00:06:55,336 --> 00:07:00,676
Some people got rich very fast, and huge parties were a way to show off new money.

97
00:07:01,077 --> 00:07:08,119
Long Island's "Gold Coast" was full of mansions, and Fitzgerald himself lived nearby while he was planning the book.

98
00:07:10,237 --> 00:07:11,617
Want to see the real thing?

99
